of independent ideas or implementation created without copying protected
expression from the Licensed Work, or is otherwise separable from the Licensed
Work under copyright law. A portion is not independently reusable merely
because it was extracted, renamed, refactored, or moved to another file.

3. SAME-LICENSE AND COMBINED-WORK CONDITIONS

A Licensee may convey or make available a Covered Work only if:

(a) The Covered Work is licensed in full under Eri License 1.0, unless a
written exception under Section 9 applies;

(b) Every copy and distributed or executable Covered Work conspicuously
includes this copyright notice and the complete Eri License 1.0 text;

(c) Corresponding Source is provided to each recipient in a complete,
restorable form under a mechanism that permits modification and redistribution;

(d) Prominent modification notices and relevant dates are included when
required; and

(e) No additional restriction is imposed on the rights granted here.

A separate and independent work is not a Covered Work merely because it is
stored beside the Licensed Work, calls the Licensed Work through a public or
undocumented API, exchanges command-line input or output, uses inter-process
communication, or communicates through a remote network protocol. The mere
availability of API interoperability does not create a Covered Work. Source
copying, protected-expression adaptation, static linking, dynamic linking
that loads or executes Licensed Work, bundling executable code, or distribution
as a combined work is covered by this Section. Whether dynamic linking creates
a Covered Work depends on whether the result contains or executes copied or
adapted protected code, not on the act of linking alone.

A container, virtual machine, serverless function, sidecar, microservice,
pod, process, plugin, dynamic module, remote worker, or isolated runtime does
not create an independent status when it contains or executes the Licensed
Work or a modified version. Routing, proxying, orchestration, batching,
tokenization, or third-party hosting does not remove the obligation.
Separate processes that communicate solely to perform one hosted service
using the Licensed Work remain part of that hosted service.

4. NETWORK SERVICE SOURCE

A Licensee that makes an unchanged or modified Covered Work available to
customers or other External Users through a public or private network must
prominently offer Corresponding Source for the complete Covered Work to every
interacting user. The offer must be available at no charge through a standard
or customary network mechanism and must include the complete source, build,
container, deployment, and modification materials required to reproduce the
covered implementation.

A hosted unchanged copy is not separately required to disclose source when
its complete source is already freely available under this License at no charge
through an established public mechanism and the hosted copy is exactly that
source plus only operational configuration. If the Licensee modifies the hosted
code or combines it with other covered code, it must offer the source of the
complete Covered Work.

This obligation applies regardless of HTTP, streaming, chunked transfer,
WebSockets, gRPC, containers, LLM or other APIs, or another protocol.

The network obligation does not apply to an independent application that only
makes ordinary requests to a separately deployed Licensed Work. Generated
text, images, data, media, model weights, model parameters, training data,
prompts, embeddings, and datasets are not Covered Works solely because they
were produced or statistically derived through use of the Licensed Work,
provided they do not contain protected expression copied from or adapted
from the Licensed Work or a Covered Work.

5. COMPLETE-COPY AND PORTION DISTRIBUTION

Complete copies, partial files, reusable portions, source archives, object
code, and complete downloads may be distributed freely when Eri License 1.0
accompanies the materials and each applicable source obligation is satisfied.
Network transfer protocols do not limit the grant. Ordinary downloading,
uploading, chunked delivery, and streaming delivery of complete restorable
software packages are permitted. Incomplete fragments may be distributed only
if the recipient receives enough complete and restorable Corresponding Source
for the distributed material and the distribution does not evade source-copy
obligations.

6. PRIVATE AND INTERNAL USE

A Licensee may use, execute, run, modify, or operate the Licensed Work
privately or internally, including for employee tools, corporate back-office
systems, payroll, logistics, data storage, batch processing, and other
commercial operations, without publishing or distributing Corresponding
Source solely because employees, contractors, or other authorized personnel
access that private operation over a network.

This private-use exception applies only while the operation is genuinely
internal and is not offered to customers or to the general public as a hosted
service, SaaS offering, API, or public-facing product. The exception covers
employees, contractors acting under the operating organization's direction,
Affiliates, and Business Partners with access limited to performing work for
that organization. A private service may be used by those participants without
source disclosure.

The operation must not be used to evade distribution obligations by allowing
an External User to remotely operate or independently obtain benefits from the
Licensed Work or a Covered Work. Merely giving a Business Partner controlled,
access-restricted access to perform contracted work is not external SaaS use.
Giving that partner independent operation rights, customer-facing operation
rights, resale rights, or an ability to offer the operation to others is
external use and triggers Section 4.

When a private service is made publicly available, sold to customers,
provided to unaffiliated partners as a hosted offering, or otherwise made
available to users outside the operating organization's internal and Affiliate
participant group, Section 4 governs and Corresponding Source must be offered
to those remote users. An internal tool that only calls an independently
deployed Licensed Work through an ordinary API and does not contain or execute
the Licensed Work remains independent.

7. COMMERCIAL USE

Commercial use, sale, support, paid distribution, and fee-based network
operation are permitted, but the right to redistribute remains available to
recipients and cannot be contracted away for the Licensed Work.

8. THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL

The Licensed Work does not grant rights in third-party code or data bundled
with it. Third-party materials remain subject to their own licenses. Eri
License applies only to the copyrightable portions owned by or properly
licensed to the applicable Eri licensors. It does not replace or restrict
third-party component licenses.

Mere aggregation in a repository, installer, archive, distribution package,
operating system, or download does not combine independent programs into a
Covered Work. A separate program remains independent if it has independent
source, purpose, authorship, and operation and is not copied, adapted,
linked, or combined with the Licensed Work as described by Section 3.

9. WRITTEN EXCEPTIONS

No exception to Sections 2 through 8 is effective unless it is explicitly
granted in writing by Nguyen Ngo or by a lawful copyright successor,
administering foundation, trust, LLC, corporation, or governance board
authorized through a public written instrument. The grant must identify the
exact exception, work, Licensee, purpose, term, and scope. Oral permission,
silence, payment, private forks, implied permission, and absence of public
notice do not grant exceptions.

A person or entity administering exceptions may not rewrite this License or
alter the rights already granted to existing compliant Licensees. It may
only make specific exceptions authorized by this Section. A commercial
exception must be documented publicly or made available to the affected
recipients through a durable record. An administering entity may be
succeeded only through a written instrument specifying lawful authority.

