contribution under Eri License 1.0 and later numbered Eri License versions;

(b) a direct grant of rights to downstream recipients under the applicable
Eri License;

(c) authority for the project administrator to enforce the license;

(d) a patent license covering claims necessarily infringed by the
contribution, when owned or controlled by the contributor and applicable to
normal use of the contribution in the Licensed Work; and

(e) an explicit statement of whether the project may grant commercial,
proprietary, or dual-license exceptions covering the contribution.

A contributor agreement may not revoke recipient rights or require an
additional license fee to exercise rights already granted. No ownership is
transferred unless the contributor signs a separate assignment identifying
that copyright interest. A dual-license grant does not require a contributor
to assign ownership.

Contributions must not secretly impose a more restrictive license or contain
third-party code under an incompatible term.

